{
  "dom_id": "step-q352372",
  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14224622/assets/dcb67e2d3fe9a413c02bb00b6d8cf7c0ac8825db1d360ab883f08baa3b6c2e9c.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by the curves $x=7-y-{y}^{2},$ $x=5-{y}^{2},$ and the $x$-axis, as shown above. Calculate the volume $V$ of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$V=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "Now, let $f(y)=7-y-{y}^{2}$ and $g(y)=5-{y}^{2}.$\n\nWe start by determining the points where the curves intersect by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =g(y) \\\\ 7-y-{y}^{2} & =5-{y}^{2} \\\\ 2-y & =0 \\\\ y & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution corresponding to our region is $y=2.$ So, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=2.$ \n\nNow, let's draw our usual horizontal arrow through the region to determine the left and right functions.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14224622/assets/cfb914bf7ed0a0c6b9f784d46a6761cd97c352fcc4e82abb29a72dc12d8ab7ca.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=g(y)=5-{y}^{2},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=f(y)=7-y-{y}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}y([\\text{right}]-[\\text{left}])\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y([7-y-{y}^{2}]-[5-{y}^{2}])\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y(2-y)\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(2y-{y}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi (2{\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{2}\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =2\\pi ({2\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}|}_{0}^{2}-{\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}|}_{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =2\\pi (2(\\frac{4}{2}-0)-(\\frac{8}{3}-0)) \\\\  & =2\\pi (4-\\frac{8}{3}) \\\\  & =2\\pi \\cdot \\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
